  <h1>India’s Health Insurance Coverage — Achievements and Gaps under Ayushman Bharat</h1>
  <div><em>Economic Times | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Access to affordable healthcare remains a key challenge in India, where out-of-pocket expenditure (OOPE) still accounts for ~50% of total health spending. In 2024, Economic Times analysed progress under Ayushman Bharat–Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (AB-PMJAY), while highlighting persisting gaps in coverage and infrastructure. For UPSC, health insurance reflects the intersection of governance, social justice, and economy.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Ayushman Bharat Coverage: ~55 crore beneficiaries (largest global health scheme).<br/>•	Insurance Penetration (2024): ~41% population covered (public + private).<br/>•	OOPE: Down from 62% (2014) to ~50% (2024).<br/>•	Private Sector Role: 70% of healthcare infrastructure outside public system.<br/>•	State Variation: Kerala, Tamil Nadu advanced; Bihar, UP lagging.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Scale of Coverage: Nearly half of India’s population under AB-PMJAY.<br/>2.	Financial Protection: ₹50,000+ crore worth of claims settled.<br/>3.	Inclusion: Focus on poor, women, and vulnerable households.<br/>4.	Digital Integration: National Health Authority (NHA) using tech for claim processing.<br/>5.	Awareness: Scheme visibility high in rural areas.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Limited Reach: 60 crore+ people remain uninsured.<br/>2.	Infrastructure Gaps: Shortage of empanelled hospitals in rural areas.<br/>3.	Quality Concerns: Uneven private hospital participation.<br/>4.	Fraud Risks: Fake claims, overbilling, and leakages.<br/>5.	Low Coverage Amount: ₹5 lakh per family insufficient for catastrophic illnesses.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	AB-PMJAY: Core of Ayushman Bharat health scheme.<br/>•	State-Specific Schemes: Aarogyasri (Telangana/AP), Karunya (Kerala).<br/>•	National Digital Health Mission (NDHM): Unified health ID and e-records.<br/>•	Health Infrastructure Mission (PM-ABHIM, 2021): ₹64,000 crore for strengthening PHCs/CHCs.<br/>•	Insurance Regulation: IRDAI reforms for broader coverage.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Social Justice: Provides health equity to poor and vulnerable.<br/>•	Economic Stability: Reduces medical bankruptcies.<br/>•	Public Health: Improves access to tertiary care.<br/>•	Global Model: One of the largest universal health coverage efforts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance & Welfare): Health policies, AB-PMJAY.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy): Social sector investment.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Health is wealth, but only if accessible to all.”<br/>o	“Universal health coverage is the foundation of inclusive growth.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: State capacity and welfare delivery.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Expand Coverage: Universal health insurance beyond poor households.<br/>2.	Strengthen Public Health Infra: Reduce over-dependence on private hospitals.<br/>3.	Enhance Package Size: Cover high-cost treatments like cancer.<br/>4.	Fraud Prevention: AI-driven claim monitoring, stricter audits.<br/>5.	Integration with Primary Care: Link insurance with Health & Wellness Centres.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Ayushman Bharat has transformed India’s health insurance landscape but must evolve toward universal, equitable, and quality healthcare. For UPSC aspirants, it highlights the link between welfare policies, governance, and human development.</p>
